  user_input {
    user_response: "Absolutely \342\200\224 based on this database report, you can turn the assignment into a **live, interactive \342\200\234Database Systems\342\200\235 showcase website** in Google AI Studio, similar to the CAPM demo but focused on ERD, normalization, entities, relationships, and live SQL-style exploration.\n\n Here is a ready-to-paste prompt:\n\n Build a polished, modern, fully interactive educational web application titled:\n\n # E-Commerce Database Design & Normalization\n\n Subtitle:\n\n **Database Systems \302\267 Semester 3 \302\267 Individual Project**\n\n Student:\n\n **Muhammad Abu Bakar (FA24-BBD-109)**\n\n Instructor:\n\n **Sir Ayyaz Mahmood**\n\n The application should transform the supplied database-design report into a professional **live database design demonstration website**.\n\n The goal is to visually demonstrate:\n\n - Conceptual database design\n- ERD modeling\n- Relational schema\n- 1NF\n- 2NF\n- 3NF\n- Primary keys\n- Foreign keys\n- Relationships\n- Cardinality\n- Entity dependencies\n- Schema transformation\n- Interactive sample data\n- Normalization before/after comparisons\n\n Do NOT make this a static portfolio page. Build an interactive database-learning/demo application where users can explore entities, relationships, normalization stages, sample records, and SQL-like queries.\n\n---\n\n # 1\\. Overall visual style\n\n Create a premium database-admin/dashboard aesthetic.\n\n Use:\n\n - Dark navy / charcoal primary theme\n- Blue and cyan accents\n- Purple accent for normalization\n- Green for primary keys\n- Orange for foreign keys\n- Red for dependency/warning indicators\n- White or light-gray text\n- Rounded cards\n- Subtle shadows\n- Smooth animations\n- Glassmorphism only where appropriate\n- Responsive design\n- Excellent mobile layout\n\n The interface should feel similar to a modern database management tool combined with an academic project presentation.\n\n Use a left sidebar on desktop and a collapsible navigation menu on mobile.\n\n Navigation:\n\n 1. Overview\n2. ERD Explorer\n3. Entities\n4. Relationships\n5. Normalization\n6. Relational Schema\n7. Data Explorer\n8. SQL Playground\n9. Project Report\n\n---\n\n # 2\\. Hero / Overview\n\n Create a dashboard landing page.\n\n Large title:\n\n **E-Commerce Database Design & Normalization**\n\n Subtitle:\n\n **1NF \302\267 2NF \302\267 3NF \302\267 ERD Modeling \302\267 Relational Design**\n\n Add a description:\n\n > Designed and normalized a relational e-commerce database covering customer management, products, inventory, orders, payments, shipment logistics, and supporting lookup entities.\n\n Create four KPI cards:\n\n ### Base Tables\n\n **9**\n\n ### Normalized Entities\n\n **19**\n\n ### Normalization\n\n **1NF \302\267 2NF \302\267 3NF**\n\n ### Relationships\n\n **16+**\n\n Add a visual transformation:\n\n **9 Base Tables \342\206\222 19 Normalized Entities**\n\n Animate the arrow.\n\n Add a prominent button:\n\n **Explore Database Design**\n\n and another:\n\n **Open ERD**\n\n---\n\n # 3\\. Important data consistency\n\n The supplied report contains some inconsistent wording, including references to 17/18 entities in some sections.\n\n The final explicit entity list contains 19 entities.\n\n Use the **19-entity final list as the authoritative schema** throughout the application.\n\n Display a small note:\n\n > The final schema contains 19 normalized entities. Earlier report sections contain references to 17/18 entities; the application uses the final 19-entity list for consistency.\n\n Do not silently change the final schema.\n\n---\n\n # 4\\. Entity architecture\n\n Use these 19 entities:\n\n ## Customer & Contact\n\n 1. Customers\n2. Customer Contact\n3. Customer Status\n4. Customer Status History\n\n ## Address\n\n 5. Address\n6. Location\n7. Customer Address\n\n ## Product Catalog\n\n 8. Category\n9. Brand\n10. Product\n\n ## Inventory & Suppliers\n\n 11. Inventory\n12. Suppliers\n\n ## Orders\n\n 13. Orders\n14. Order Financials\n15. Order Items\n\n ## Payments\n\n 16. Payments\n17. Payment Method\n18. Payment Status\n\n ## Shipping\n\n 19. Shipments\n20. Carriers\n21. Service Levels\n\n IMPORTANT:\n\n The report\'s final list actually enumerates 19 names but omits Inventory and Suppliers from that final list despite discussing them earlier.\n\n Resolve this explicitly in the UI:\n\n - Treat Inventory and Suppliers as part of the complete logical schema because they are documented entities and have defined relationships.\n- Display a schema count of **21 documented entities** if Inventory and Suppliers are included.\n- Alternatively, create a toggle:\n  - **Final listed entities: 19**\n  - **Complete documented schema: 21**\n\n Clearly explain this discrepancy.\n\n Do not invent additional entities.\n\n---\n\n # 5\\. ERD Explorer\n\n This should be the centerpiece of the application.\n\n Create an interactive ERD canvas.\n\n Display entities as draggable cards.\n\n Each entity should show:\n\n - Entity name\n- Primary key\n- Foreign keys\n- Important attributes\n\n Use connecting lines for relationships.\n\n Color-code:\n\n - Green = Primary Key\n- Orange = Foreign Key\n- Blue = normal attribute\n- Purple = lookup/reference entity\n\n Users should be able to:\n\n - Drag entities\n- Zoom in/out\n- Pan\n- Reset layout\n- Search for an entity\n- Click an entity to open its details\n- Highlight connected entities\n- Hide/show relationship labels\n\n Add controls:\n\n **Zoom +**\n\n **Zoom \342\210\222**\n\n **Fit Diagram**\n\n **Reset Layout**\n\n **Hide Relationships**\n\n **Show All**\n\n---\n\n # 6\\. ERD entity details\n\n When an entity is clicked, open a right-side details drawer.\n\n Show:\n\n ### Entity Name\n\n ### Purpose\n\n ### Primary Key\n\n ### Foreign Keys\n\n ### Attributes\n\n ### Relationships\n\n ### Cardinality\n\n ### Example record\n\n For example:\n\n ## Customers\n\n Purpose:\n\n Stores core customer information.\n\n Primary key:\n\n `CustomerID`\n\n Attributes:\n\n - CustomerID\n- Name\n- CreatedAt\n\n Relationships:\n\n - Customers \342\206\222 Orders = 1:M\n- Customers \342\206\222 Customer Contact = 1:M\n- Customers \342\206\222 Customer Status = 1:1\n- Customers \342\206\222 Customer Status History = 1:M\n\n---\n\n # 7\\. Complete relationship dataset\n\n Implement these relationships:\n\n | Parent | Child | Cardinality | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Customers | Orders | 1:M | A customer can place multiple orders |\n| Customers | Customer Contact | 1:M | A customer can have multiple contacts |\n| Customers | Customer Status | 1:1 | A customer has one current status |\n| Customers | Customer Status History | 1:M | Historical status entries per customer |\n| Location | Address | 1:1 | A location maps to an address |\n| Location | Customer Address | 1:M | A location can be linked to multiple customer addresses |\n| Orders | Order Items | 1:M | An order contains multiple items |\n| Orders | Order Financials | 1:1 | One financial record per order |\n| Orders | Payments | 1:M | Multiple payments may apply to an order |\n| Orders | Shipments | 1:M | An order can generate multiple shipments |\n| Products | Inventory | 1:M | A product may appear in multiple inventory entries |\n| Inventory | Suppliers | M:1 | Each inventory record belongs to one supplier |\n| Payments | Payment Method | M:1 | Each payment uses one method |\n| Payments | Payment Status | M:1 | Each payment has one status |\n| Shipments | Carriers | M:1 | Each shipment uses one carrier |\n| Shipments | Service Levels | M:1 | Each shipment uses one service level |\n\nMake relationship lines clickable.\n\n When a line is clicked, show:\n\n **Parent \342\206\222 Child**\n\n **Cardinality**\n\n **Business rule**\n\n---\n\n # 8\\. Entity browser\n\n Create a searchable entity table.\n\n Columns:\n\n | Entity | Domain | Primary Key | Foreign Keys | Relationships |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nAdd filters:\n\n - Customer\n- Address\n- Product\n- Inventory\n- Supplier\n- Orders\n- Payments\n- Shipping\n- Lookup\n\n Add a search box:\n\n **Search entities...**\n\n Clicking an entity should open its ERD details.\n\n---\n\n # 9\\. Normalization interactive section\n\n Create a major section:\n\n # Normalization Lab\n\n Explain:\n\n **1NF \342\206\222 2NF \342\206\222 3NF**\n\n Create an interactive step selector.\n\n---\n\n ## 1NF\n\n Explain:\n\n > First Normal Form removes repeating groups and ensures attributes contain atomic values.\n\n Show an example of the original problem:\n\n ### Before 1NF\n\n```\nOrderID\nCustomer\nProduct1\nQty1\nProduct2\nQty2\nProduct3\nQty3\n```\n\n Show the problem:\n\n **Repeating groups**\n\n Then show:\n\n ### After 1NF\n\n```\nOrders\nOrderID\nCustomerID\n\nOrderItems\nOrderItemID\nOrderID\nProductID\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n Animate the transformation.\n\n---\n\n # 10\\. 2NF visualization\n\n Explain:\n\n > Second Normal Form removes partial dependencies from tables with composite keys.\n\n Create an interactive dependency diagram.\n\n Example:\n\n```\nOrderID + ProductID\n        |\n        +---- Quantity\n        +---- UnitPrice\n```\n\n Explain that product-specific ordering attributes belong to Order Items.\n\n Show:\n\n ### Before\n\n```\nOrderID\nProductID\nProductName\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n ### After\n\n```\nOrders\nOrderID\nCustomerID\nOrderDate\n\nOrderItems\nOrderItemID\nOrderID\nProductID\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n Highlight the dependency movement.\n\n---\n\n # 11\\. 3NF visualization\n\n Explain:\n\n > Third Normal Form removes transitive dependencies where a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute.\n\n Use an interactive example.\n\n Before:\n\n```\nProductID\nProductName\nCategoryID\nCategoryName\nBrandID\nBrandName\n```\n\n Highlight:\n\n```\nProductID \342\206\222 CategoryID \342\206\222 CategoryName\nProductID \342\206\222 BrandID \342\206\222 BrandName\n```\n\n Then transform into:\n\n```\nCategory\nCategoryID\nCategoryName\n\nBrand\nBrandID\nBrandName\n\nProduct\nProductID\nCategoryID\nBrandID\nProductName\nSKU\nPrice\nCost\nActive\n```\n\n Use animated arrows.\n\n---\n\n # 12\\. Normalization journey\n\n Create a visual timeline:\n\n **Original 9 Base Tables**\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **1NF**\n\n Remove repeating groups.\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **2NF**\n\n Remove partial dependencies.\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **3NF**\n\n Remove transitive dependencies.\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **Final normalized relational design**\n\n Add a progress animation.\n\n Allow the user to click each stage.\n\n---\n\n # 13\\. Base-table explorer\n\n Create a section:\n\n # From 9 Base Tables to Normalized Entities\n\n Cards:\n\n 1. Customers\n2. Address\n3. Products\n4. Inventory\n5. Suppliers\n6. Orders\n7. Order Items\n8. Payments\n9. Shipments\n\n When clicked, show:\n\n - Original purpose\n- 1NF transformation\n- 2NF transformation\n- 3NF transformation\n- Final entities\n\n Example:\n\n ### Customers\n\n Original:\n\n Customers contained customer details, contacts, and status information.\n\n 1NF:\n\n Separate repeating customer contacts and status history.\n\n 2NF:\n\n No partial dependency because CustomerID is a single key.\n\n 3NF:\n\n Separate concepts into:\n\n - Customers\n- Customer Contact\n- Customer Status\n- Customer Status History\n\n Show the transformation visually:\n\n **Customers**\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **Customers**\n\n **Customer Contact**\n\n **Customer Status**\n\n **Customer Status History**\n\n---\n\n # 14\\. Relational schema section\n\n Create a professional schema browser.\n\n For each table display:\n\n```\nTABLE Customers\n\nPK  CustomerID\n    Name\n    CreatedAt\n```\n\n For Product:\n\n```\nTABLE Product\n\nPK  ProductID\nFK  CategoryID\nFK  BrandID\n    SKU\n    ProductName\n    Price\n    Cost\n    Active\n```\n\n For Orders:\n\n```\nTABLE Orders\n\nPK  OrderID\nFK  CustomerID\n    OrderDate\n    Status\n```\n\n For Order Items:\n\n```\nTABLE OrderItems\n\nPK  OrderItemID\nFK  OrderID\nFK  ProductID\n    Quantity\n    UnitPrice\n```\n\n For Payments:\n\n```\nTABLE Payments\n\nPK  PaymentID\nFK  OrderID\nFK  MethodID\nFK  StatusID\n    Amount\n    GatewayRef\n    AuthCode\n```\n\n For Shipments:\n\n```\nTABLE Shipments\n\nPK  ShipmentID\nFK  OrderID\nFK  CarrierID\nFK  ServiceLevelID\n    TrackingNo\n    ShipDate\n    DeliveryDate\n```\n\n Include all documented entities.\n\n---\n\n # 15\\. Data Explorer\n\n Create a simulated database browser.\n\n Tabs:\n\n - Customers\n- Products\n- Orders\n- Order Items\n- Payments\n- Shipments\n- Suppliers\n- Inventory\n\n Display realistic sample data derived from the report.\n\n Examples:\n\n ### Customers\n\n | CustomerID | Name | CreatedAt |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Hira Raza | 2024-11-12 |\n| 2 | Usman Yousaf | 2024-10-14 |\n| 3 | Hira Yousaf | 2025-06-14 |\n\n### Customer Contact\n\n | ContactID | CustomerID | Phone | Email |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 1 | +92 321 2188789 | hira.raza@gmail.com |\n| 2 | 2 | +92 334 3219824 | usmanyousaf@gmail.com |\n| 3 | 3 | +92 312 2582524 | hira.yousaf@retail.pk |\n\n### Category\n\n | CategoryID | CategoryName |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Smartphones |\n| 2 | Laptops |\n| 3 | Accessories |\n\n### Brand\n\n | BrandID | BrandName |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Samsung |\n| 2 | Apple |\n| 3 | HP |\n\n### Product\n\n Include:\n\n - Samsung S24 Ultra\n- iPhone 15 Pro Max\n- HP Victus 15\n\n with the supplied example SKU, price, cost, category, brand and active values.\n\n ### Payment Method\n\n - Card\n- COD\n- Wallet\n\n ### Payment Status\n\n - Pending\n- Captured\n\n ### Carriers\n\n - Leopard\n- BlueEx\n- TCS\n\n ### Service Levels\n\n - Standard\n- Express\n\n Do not fabricate large quantities of supposedly real data. Clearly label generated/demo rows as **Sample Data**.\n\n---\n\n # 16\\. SQL Playground\n\n Create an interactive SQL-style playground.\n\n Users can type simple SELECT queries.\n\n Provide preset queries:\n\n ### Query 1\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM Customers;\n```\n\n ### Query 2\n\n```\nSELECT ProductName, Price\nFROM Product\nWHERE Price > 500;\n```\n\n ### Query 3\n\n```\nSELECT *\nFROM Orders\nWHERE CustomerID = 1;\n```\n\n ### Query 4\n\n```\nSELECT ProductName, CategoryName\nFROM Product\nJOIN Category\nON Product.CategoryID = Category.CategoryID;\n```\n\n ### Query 5\n\n```\nSELECT OrderID, Amount\nFROM Payments\nWHERE StatusID = 2;\n```\n\n The playground does not need a real database server.\n\n Implement a lightweight in-browser query engine supporting at minimum:\n\n - SELECT\n- FROM\n- WHERE\n- simple AND/OR\n- ORDER BY\n- LIMIT\n- INNER JOIN\n\n If implementing a complete SQL engine is impractical, provide a controlled parser for the supported examples and clearly display:\n\n **Demo SQL Engine \342\200\224 Browser Only**\n\n Add:\n\n **Run Query**\n\n **Clear**\n\n **Load Example**\n\n Show query execution time.\n\n---\n\n # 17\\. Dependency Explorer\n\n Create a section called:\n\n # Functional Dependency Explorer\n\n Allow users to select an entity.\n\n Example for Product:\n\n```\nProductID\n   \342\206\223\nProductName\nSKU\nPrice\nCost\nActive\nCategoryID\nBrandID\n```\n\n Then show:\n\n```\nCategoryID\n   \342\206\223\nCategoryName\n```\n\n```\nBrandID\n   \342\206\223\nBrandName\n```\n\n Explain why CategoryName and BrandName should not be stored directly in Product.\n\n For Orders:\n\n```\nOrderID\n   \342\206\223\nCustomerID\nOrderDate\nStatus\n```\n\n For Order Items:\n\n```\nOrderItemID\n   \342\206\223\nOrderID\nProductID\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n Make dependencies visually animated.\n\n---\n\n # 18\\. Cardinality legend\n\n Create a reusable legend:\n\n - **1:1** One-to-One\n- **1:M** One-to-Many\n- **M:1** Many-to-One\n- **M:M** Many-to-Many\n\n Explain each with a simple e-commerce example.\n\n---\n\n # 19\\. Database statistics dashboard\n\n Add live statistics cards:\n\n ### Entities\n\n 19 / 21 depending on selected schema view\n\n ### Base Tables\n\n 9\n\n ### Normalization Levels\n\n 3\n\n ### PKs\n\n Count dynamically\n\n ### FKs\n\n Count dynamically\n\n ### Relationships\n\n Count dynamically\n\n ### Lookup Entities\n\n 6+\n\n Create a small chart showing entity distribution by domain:\n\n - Customer\n- Address\n- Catalog\n- Inventory\n- Orders\n- Payments\n- Shipping\n\n---\n\n # 20\\. Project report section\n\n Create a polished report summary.\n\n Sections:\n\n ### Executive Summary\n\n Summarize the project:\n\n The database was designed for an e-commerce system covering customers, products, inventory, orders, payments and shipment logistics.\n\n The original design was normalized through 1NF, 2NF and 3NF to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity.\n\n ### Design Goals\n\n - Organize data logically\n- Reduce redundancy\n- Prevent update anomalies\n- Maintain referential integrity\n- Support scalable reporting\n- Provide a clear relational blueprint\n\n ### Final Outcome\n\n Explain the transformation from the original base-table design to the normalized relational model.\n\n---\n\n # 21\\. Data-quality / report consistency panel\n\n Create a small warning panel:\n\n ## Report Consistency Notes\n\n The submitted report contains some numerical/entity-count inconsistencies:\n\n 1. Some sections say 17 or 18 entities.\n2. The final list explicitly names 19 entities.\n3. Inventory and Suppliers are described as entities but are omitted from the final 19-item list.\n4. The conclusion references \342\200\23417\342\200\22318 entities.\342\200\235\n\n The application should distinguish:\n\n **Final Explicit List: 19**\n\n and\n\n **Complete Documented Schema: 21 including Inventory and Suppliers**\n\n Do not hide this discrepancy.\n\n---\n\n # 22\\. Interactive relationship highlighting\n\n When the user clicks an entity such as:\n\n **Orders**\n\n highlight:\n\n - Customers\n- Order Items\n- Order Financials\n- Payments\n- Shipments\n\n Dim all unrelated entities.\n\n Display:\n\n```\nCustomers\n   \342\224\202\n   \342\224\202 1:M\n   \342\226\274\nOrders\n \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:M \342\206\222 Order Items\n \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:1 \342\206\222 Order Financials\n \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:M \342\206\222 Payments\n \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:M \342\206\222 Shipments\n```\n\n This should be one of the strongest visual features of the application.\n\n---\n\n # 23\\. Search\n\n Add global search.\n\n Placeholder:\n\n **Search entities, attributes, relationships...**\n\n Searching:\n\n `Payment`\n\n should return:\n\n - Payments\n- Payment Method\n- Payment Status\n- Orders \342\206\222 Payments relationship\n\n Searching:\n\n `CustomerID`\n\n should return every table containing CustomerID.\n\n---\n\n # 24\\. Presentation mode\n\n Add a button:\n\n **Presentation Mode**\n\n When activated:\n\n - Hide sidebar\n- Expand ERD/chart\n- Increase font sizes\n- Use full screen\n- Provide Previous / Next controls\n\n Presentation sections:\n\n 1. Project Overview\n2. Original 9 Tables\n3. 1NF\n4. 2NF\n5. 3NF\n6. Final Schema\n7. ERD\n8. Sample Data\n9. Conclusion\n\n This allows the website to be used during a university viva/presentation.\n\n---\n\n # 25\\. Conclusion section\n\n Create:\n\n ## Conclusion\n\n Explain that the normalized design separates customer management, address management, product cataloging, inventory, orders, payments and logistics into logical entities.\n\n Explain that normalization:\n\n - removes repeating groups\n- reduces redundancy\n- removes partial dependencies\n- removes transitive dependencies\n- improves consistency\n- strengthens referential integrity\n- makes the database easier to maintain and extend\n\n End with:\n\n **9 Base Tables \342\206\222 Normalized Relational Design**\n\n---\n\n # 26\\. Footer\n\n Display:\n\n **Database Systems \302\267 Semester 3 \302\267 Individual Project**\n\n **Muhammad Abu Bakar (FA24-BBD-109)**\n\n **Prepared for Sir Ayyaz Mahmood**\n\n **Educational Database Design Demonstration**\n\n---\n\n # 27\\. Technical requirements\n\n Build the complete working frontend.\n\n Prefer:\n\n - React\n- TypeScript\n- modern CSS\n- SVG for ERD\n- client-side state management\n\n Do not require a backend.\n\n All interactive functionality should work immediately after the app loads.\n\n Do not use fake buttons.\n\n Every button must perform an action.\n\n Implement:\n\n - Entity search\n- Entity selection\n- ERD highlighting\n- Zoom\n- Pan\n- Relationship filtering\n- Normalization stage switching\n- Data table filtering\n- SQL demo execution\n- Dark mode\n- Presentation mode\n- Reset layout\n- Responsive navigation\n\n Use clean component architecture.\n\n Create reusable components such as:\n\n - `Sidebar`\n- `Header`\n- `StatCard`\n- `EntityCard`\n- `ERDCanvas`\n- `RelationshipLine`\n- `EntityDrawer`\n- `NormalizationStep`\n- `SchemaTable`\n- `DataExplorer`\n- `SQLPlayground`\n- `DependencyGraph`\n- `SearchBar`\n\n---\n\n # 28\\. Final quality requirements\n\n Before completing the application, test:\n\n 1. The application loads without errors.\n2. The ERD renders correctly.\n3. Entities can be selected.\n4. Relationships highlight correctly.\n5. Search works.\n6. Entity filtering works.\n7. Normalization steps switch correctly.\n8. Sample data tables work.\n9. SQL examples execute.\n10. Dark mode works.\n11. Presentation mode works.\n12. Mobile layout works.\n13. No dead buttons exist.\n14. No console errors exist.\n15. The 19-vs-21 entity discrepancy is clearly documented.\n16. All relationships from the supplied report are represented.\n17. Primary keys and foreign keys are visually distinguishable.\n18. The website looks like a polished database product rather than a plain academic HTML page.\n\n Most importantly, make the **ERD Explorer + Normalization Lab + SQL Playground** the centerpiece of the experience.\n\n The finished result should feel like an interactive database system that demonstrates the student\'s understanding of relational database design, rather than merely displaying the contents of the report."
    items {
      text: "Absolutely \342\200\224 based on this database report, you can turn the assignment into a **live, interactive \342\200\234Database Systems\342\200\235 showcase website** in Google AI Studio, similar to the CAPM demo but focused on ERD, normalization, entities, relationships, and live SQL-style exploration.\n\n Here is a ready-to-paste prompt:\n\n Build a polished, modern, fully interactive educational web application titled:\n\n # E-Commerce Database Design & Normalization\n\n Subtitle:\n\n **Database Systems \302\267 Semester 3 \302\267 Individual Project**\n\n Student:\n\n **Muhammad Abu Bakar (FA24-BBD-109)**\n\n Instructor:\n\n **Sir Ayyaz Mahmood**\n\n The application should transform the supplied database-design report into a professional **live database design demonstration website**.\n\n The goal is to visually demonstrate:\n\n - Conceptual database design\n- ERD modeling\n- Relational schema\n- 1NF\n- 2NF\n- 3NF\n- Primary keys\n- Foreign keys\n- Relationships\n- Cardinality\n- Entity dependencies\n- Schema transformation\n- Interactive sample data\n- Normalization before/after comparisons\n\n Do NOT make this a static portfolio page. Build an interactive database-learning/demo application where users can explore entities, relationships, normalization stages, sample records, and SQL-like queries.\n\n---\n\n # 1\\. Overall visual style\n\n Create a premium database-admin/dashboard aesthetic.\n\n Use:\n\n - Dark navy / charcoal primary theme\n- Blue and cyan accents\n- Purple accent for normalization\n- Green for primary keys\n- Orange for foreign keys\n- Red for dependency/warning indicators\n- White or light-gray text\n- Rounded cards\n- Subtle shadows\n- Smooth animations\n- Glassmorphism only where appropriate\n- Responsive design\n- Excellent mobile layout\n\n The interface should feel similar to a modern database management tool combined with an academic project presentation.\n\n Use a left sidebar on desktop and a collapsible navigation menu on mobile.\n\n Navigation:\n\n 1. Overview\n2. ERD Explorer\n3. Entities\n4. Relationships\n5. Normalization\n6. Relational Schema\n7. Data Explorer\n8. SQL Playground\n9. Project Report\n\n---\n\n # 2\\. Hero / Overview\n\n Create a dashboard landing page.\n\n Large title:\n\n **E-Commerce Database Design & Normalization**\n\n Subtitle:\n\n **1NF \302\267 2NF \302\267 3NF \302\267 ERD Modeling \302\267 Relational Design**\n\n Add a description:\n\n > Designed and normalized a relational e-commerce database covering customer management, products, inventory, orders, payments, shipment logistics, and supporting lookup entities.\n\n Create four KPI cards:\n\n ### Base Tables\n\n **9**\n\n ### Normalized Entities\n\n **19**\n\n ### Normalization\n\n **1NF \302\267 2NF \302\267 3NF**\n\n ### Relationships\n\n **16+**\n\n Add a visual transformation:\n\n **9 Base Tables \342\206\222 19 Normalized Entities**\n\n Animate the arrow.\n\n Add a prominent button:\n\n **Explore Database Design**\n\n and another:\n\n **Open ERD**\n\n---\n\n # 3\\. Important data consistency\n\n The supplied report contains some inconsistent wording, including references to 17/18 entities in some sections.\n\n The final explicit entity list contains 19 entities.\n\n Use the **19-entity final list as the authoritative schema** throughout the application.\n\n Display a small note:\n\n > The final schema contains 19 normalized entities. Earlier report sections contain references to 17/18 entities; the application uses the final 19-entity list for consistency.\n\n Do not silently change the final schema.\n\n---\n\n # 4\\. Entity architecture\n\n Use these 19 entities:\n\n ## Customer & Contact\n\n 1. Customers\n2. Customer Contact\n3. Customer Status\n4. Customer Status History\n\n ## Address\n\n 5. Address\n6. Location\n7. Customer Address\n\n ## Product Catalog\n\n 8. Category\n9. Brand\n10. Product\n\n ## Inventory & Suppliers\n\n 11. Inventory\n12. Suppliers\n\n ## Orders\n\n 13. Orders\n14. Order Financials\n15. Order Items\n\n ## Payments\n\n 16. Payments\n17. Payment Method\n18. Payment Status\n\n ## Shipping\n\n 19. Shipments\n20. Carriers\n21. Service Levels\n\n IMPORTANT:\n\n The report\'s final list actually enumerates 19 names but omits Inventory and Suppliers from that final list despite discussing them earlier.\n\n Resolve this explicitly in the UI:\n\n - Treat Inventory and Suppliers as part of the complete logical schema because they are documented entities and have defined relationships.\n- Display a schema count of **21 documented entities** if Inventory and Suppliers are included.\n- Alternatively, create a toggle:\n  - **Final listed entities: 19**\n  - **Complete documented schema: 21**\n\n Clearly explain this discrepancy.\n\n Do not invent additional entities.\n\n---\n\n # 5\\. ERD Explorer\n\n This should be the centerpiece of the application.\n\n Create an interactive ERD canvas.\n\n Display entities as draggable cards.\n\n Each entity should show:\n\n - Entity name\n- Primary key\n- Foreign keys\n- Important attributes\n\n Use connecting lines for relationships.\n\n Color-code:\n\n - Green = Primary Key\n- Orange = Foreign Key\n- Blue = normal attribute\n- Purple = lookup/reference entity\n\n Users should be able to:\n\n - Drag entities\n- Zoom in/out\n- Pan\n- Reset layout\n- Search for an entity\n- Click an entity to open its details\n- Highlight connected entities\n- Hide/show relationship labels\n\n Add controls:\n\n **Zoom +**\n\n **Zoom \342\210\222**\n\n **Fit Diagram**\n\n **Reset Layout**\n\n **Hide Relationships**\n\n **Show All**\n\n---\n\n # 6\\. ERD entity details\n\n When an entity is clicked, open a right-side details drawer.\n\n Show:\n\n ### Entity Name\n\n ### Purpose\n\n ### Primary Key\n\n ### Foreign Keys\n\n ### Attributes\n\n ### Relationships\n\n ### Cardinality\n\n ### Example record\n\n For example:\n\n ## Customers\n\n Purpose:\n\n Stores core customer information.\n\n Primary key:\n\n `CustomerID`\n\n Attributes:\n\n - CustomerID\n- Name\n- CreatedAt\n\n Relationships:\n\n - Customers \342\206\222 Orders = 1:M\n- Customers \342\206\222 Customer Contact = 1:M\n- Customers \342\206\222 Customer Status = 1:1\n- Customers \342\206\222 Customer Status History = 1:M\n\n---\n\n # 7\\. Complete relationship dataset\n\n Implement these relationships:\n\n | Parent | Child | Cardinality | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Customers | Orders | 1:M | A customer can place multiple orders |\n| Customers | Customer Contact | 1:M | A customer can have multiple contacts |\n| Customers | Customer Status | 1:1 | A customer has one current status |\n| Customers | Customer Status History | 1:M | Historical status entries per customer |\n| Location | Address | 1:1 | A location maps to an address |\n| Location | Customer Address | 1:M | A location can be linked to multiple customer addresses |\n| Orders | Order Items | 1:M | An order contains multiple items |\n| Orders | Order Financials | 1:1 | One financial record per order |\n| Orders | Payments | 1:M | Multiple payments may apply to an order |\n| Orders | Shipments | 1:M | An order can generate multiple shipments |\n| Products | Inventory | 1:M | A product may appear in multiple inventory entries |\n| Inventory | Suppliers | M:1 | Each inventory record belongs to one supplier |\n| Payments | Payment Method | M:1 | Each payment uses one method |\n| Payments | Payment Status | M:1 | Each payment has one status |\n| Shipments | Carriers | M:1 | Each shipment uses one carrier |\n| Shipments | Service Levels | M:1 | Each shipment uses one service level |\n\nMake relationship lines clickable.\n\n When a line is clicked, show:\n\n **Parent \342\206\222 Child**\n\n **Cardinality**\n\n **Business rule**\n\n---\n\n # 8\\. Entity browser\n\n Create a searchable entity table.\n\n Columns:\n\n | Entity | Domain | Primary Key | Foreign Keys | Relationships |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nAdd filters:\n\n - Customer\n- Address\n- Product\n- Inventory\n- Supplier\n- Orders\n- Payments\n- Shipping\n- Lookup\n\n Add a search box:\n\n **Search entities...**\n\n Clicking an entity should open its ERD details.\n\n---\n\n # 9\\. Normalization interactive section\n\n Create a major section:\n\n # Normalization Lab\n\n Explain:\n\n **1NF \342\206\222 2NF \342\206\222 3NF**\n\n Create an interactive step selector.\n\n---\n\n ## 1NF\n\n Explain:\n\n > First Normal Form removes repeating groups and ensures attributes contain atomic values.\n\n Show an example of the original problem:\n\n ### Before 1NF\n\n```\nOrderID\nCustomer\nProduct1\nQty1\nProduct2\nQty2\nProduct3\nQty3\n```\n\n Show the problem:\n\n **Repeating groups**\n\n Then show:\n\n ### After 1NF\n\n```\nOrders\nOrderID\nCustomerID\n\nOrderItems\nOrderItemID\nOrderID\nProductID\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n Animate the transformation.\n\n---\n\n # 10\\. 2NF visualization\n\n Explain:\n\n > Second Normal Form removes partial dependencies from tables with composite keys.\n\n Create an interactive dependency diagram.\n\n Example:\n\n```\nOrderID + ProductID\n        |\n        +---- Quantity\n        +---- UnitPrice\n```\n\n Explain that product-specific ordering attributes belong to Order Items.\n\n Show:\n\n ### Before\n\n```\nOrderID\nProductID\nProductName\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n ### After\n\n```\nOrders\nOrderID\nCustomerID\nOrderDate\n\nOrderItems\nOrderItemID\nOrderID\nProductID\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n Highlight the dependency movement.\n\n---\n\n # 11\\. 3NF visualization\n\n Explain:\n\n > Third Normal Form removes transitive dependencies where a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute.\n\n Use an interactive example.\n\n Before:\n\n```\nProductID\nProductName\nCategoryID\nCategoryName\nBrandID\nBrandName\n```\n\n Highlight:\n\n```\nProductID \342\206\222 CategoryID \342\206\222 CategoryName\nProductID \342\206\222 BrandID \342\206\222 BrandName\n```\n\n Then transform into:\n\n```\nCategory\nCategoryID\nCategoryName\n\nBrand\nBrandID\nBrandName\n\nProduct\nProductID\nCategoryID\nBrandID\nProductName\nSKU\nPrice\nCost\nActive\n```\n\n Use animated arrows.\n\n---\n\n # 12\\. Normalization journey\n\n Create a visual timeline:\n\n **Original 9 Base Tables**\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **1NF**\n\n Remove repeating groups.\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **2NF**\n\n Remove partial dependencies.\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **3NF**\n\n Remove transitive dependencies.\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **Final normalized relational design**\n\n Add a progress animation.\n\n Allow the user to click each stage.\n\n---\n\n # 13\\. Base-table explorer\n\n Create a section:\n\n # From 9 Base Tables to Normalized Entities\n\n Cards:\n\n 1. Customers\n2. Address\n3. Products\n4. Inventory\n5. Suppliers\n6. Orders\n7. Order Items\n8. Payments\n9. Shipments\n\n When clicked, show:\n\n - Original purpose\n- 1NF transformation\n- 2NF transformation\n- 3NF transformation\n- Final entities\n\n Example:\n\n ### Customers\n\n Original:\n\n Customers contained customer details, contacts, and status information.\n\n 1NF:\n\n Separate repeating customer contacts and status history.\n\n 2NF:\n\n No partial dependency because CustomerID is a single key.\n\n 3NF:\n\n Separate concepts into:\n\n - Customers\n- Customer Contact\n- Customer Status\n- Customer Status History\n\n Show the transformation visually:\n\n **Customers**\n\n \342\206\223\n\n **Customers**\n\n **Customer Contact**\n\n **Customer Status**\n\n **Customer Status History**\n\n---\n\n # 14\\. Relational schema section\n\n Create a professional schema browser.\n\n For each table display:\n\n```\nTABLE Customers\n\nPK  CustomerID\n    Name\n    CreatedAt\n```\n\n For Product:\n\n```\nTABLE Product\n\nPK  ProductID\nFK  CategoryID\nFK  BrandID\n    SKU\n    ProductName\n    Price\n    Cost\n    Active\n```\n\n For Orders:\n\n```\nTABLE Orders\n\nPK  OrderID\nFK  CustomerID\n    OrderDate\n    Status\n```\n\n For Order Items:\n\n```\nTABLE OrderItems\n\nPK  OrderItemID\nFK  OrderID\nFK  ProductID\n    Quantity\n    UnitPrice\n```\n\n For Payments:\n\n```\nTABLE Payments\n\nPK  PaymentID\nFK  OrderID\nFK  MethodID\nFK  StatusID\n    Amount\n    GatewayRef\n    AuthCode\n```\n\n For Shipments:\n\n```\nTABLE Shipments\n\nPK  ShipmentID\nFK  OrderID\nFK  CarrierID\nFK  ServiceLevelID\n    TrackingNo\n    ShipDate\n    DeliveryDate\n```\n\n Include all documented entities.\n\n---\n\n # 15\\. Data Explorer\n\n Create a simulated database browser.\n\n Tabs:\n\n - Customers\n- Products\n- Orders\n- Order Items\n- Payments\n- Shipments\n- Suppliers\n- Inventory\n\n Display realistic sample data derived from the report.\n\n Examples:\n\n ### Customers\n\n | CustomerID | Name | CreatedAt |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Hira Raza | 2024-11-12 |\n| 2 | Usman Yousaf | 2024-10-14 |\n| 3 | Hira Yousaf | 2025-06-14 |\n\n### Customer Contact\n\n | ContactID | CustomerID | Phone | Email |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 1 | +92 321 2188789 | hira.raza@gmail.com |\n| 2 | 2 | +92 334 3219824 | usmanyousaf@gmail.com |\n| 3 | 3 | +92 312 2582524 | hira.yousaf@retail.pk |\n\n### Category\n\n | CategoryID | CategoryName |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Smartphones |\n| 2 | Laptops |\n| 3 | Accessories |\n\n### Brand\n\n | BrandID | BrandName |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Samsung |\n| 2 | Apple |\n| 3 | HP |\n\n### Product\n\n Include:\n\n - Samsung S24 Ultra\n- iPhone 15 Pro Max\n- HP Victus 15\n\n with the supplied example SKU, price, cost, category, brand and active values.\n\n ### Payment Method\n\n - Card\n- COD\n- Wallet\n\n ### Payment Status\n\n - Pending\n- Captured\n\n ### Carriers\n\n - Leopard\n- BlueEx\n- TCS\n\n ### Service Levels\n\n - Standard\n- Express\n\n Do not fabricate large quantities of supposedly real data. Clearly label generated/demo rows as **Sample Data**.\n\n---\n\n # 16\\. SQL Playground\n\n Create an interactive SQL-style playground.\n\n Users can type simple SELECT queries.\n\n Provide preset queries:\n\n ### Query 1\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM Customers;\n```\n\n ### Query 2\n\n```\nSELECT ProductName, Price\nFROM Product\nWHERE Price > 500;\n```\n\n ### Query 3\n\n```\nSELECT *\nFROM Orders\nWHERE CustomerID = 1;\n```\n\n ### Query 4\n\n```\nSELECT ProductName, CategoryName\nFROM Product\nJOIN Category\nON Product.CategoryID = Category.CategoryID;\n```\n\n ### Query 5\n\n```\nSELECT OrderID, Amount\nFROM Payments\nWHERE StatusID = 2;\n```\n\n The playground does not need a real database server.\n\n Implement a lightweight in-browser query engine supporting at minimum:\n\n - SELECT\n- FROM\n- WHERE\n- simple AND/OR\n- ORDER BY\n- LIMIT\n- INNER JOIN\n\n If implementing a complete SQL engine is impractical, provide a controlled parser for the supported examples and clearly display:\n\n **Demo SQL Engine \342\200\224 Browser Only**\n\n Add:\n\n **Run Query**\n\n **Clear**\n\n **Load Example**\n\n Show query execution time.\n\n---\n\n # 17\\. Dependency Explorer\n\n Create a section called:\n\n # Functional Dependency Explorer\n\n Allow users to select an entity.\n\n Example for Product:\n\n```\nProductID\n   \342\206\223\nProductName\nSKU\nPrice\nCost\nActive\nCategoryID\nBrandID\n```\n\n Then show:\n\n```\nCategoryID\n   \342\206\223\nCategoryName\n```\n\n```\nBrandID\n   \342\206\223\nBrandName\n```\n\n Explain why CategoryName and BrandName should not be stored directly in Product.\n\n For Orders:\n\n```\nOrderID\n   \342\206\223\nCustomerID\nOrderDate\nStatus\n```\n\n For Order Items:\n\n```\nOrderItemID\n   \342\206\223\nOrderID\nProductID\nQuantity\nUnitPrice\n```\n\n Make dependencies visually animated.\n\n---\n\n # 18\\. Cardinality legend\n\n Create a reusable legend:\n\n - **1:1** One-to-One\n- **1:M** One-to-Many\n- **M:1** Many-to-One\n- **M:M** Many-to-Many\n\n Explain each with a simple e-commerce example.\n\n---\n\n # 19\\. Database statistics dashboard\n\n Add live statistics cards:\n\n ### Entities\n\n 19 / 21 depending on selected schema view\n\n ### Base Tables\n\n 9\n\n ### Normalization Levels\n\n 3\n\n ### PKs\n\n Count dynamically\n\n ### FKs\n\n Count dynamically\n\n ### Relationships\n\n Count dynamically\n\n ### Lookup Entities\n\n 6+\n\n Create a small chart showing entity distribution by domain:\n\n - Customer\n- Address\n- Catalog\n- Inventory\n- Orders\n- Payments\n- Shipping\n\n---\n\n # 20\\. Project report section\n\n Create a polished report summary.\n\n Sections:\n\n ### Executive Summary\n\n Summarize the project:\n\n The database was designed for an e-commerce system covering customers, products, inventory, orders, payments and shipment logistics.\n\n The original design was normalized through 1NF, 2NF and 3NF to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity.\n\n ### Design Goals\n\n - Organize data logically\n- Reduce redundancy\n- Prevent update anomalies\n- Maintain referential integrity\n- Support scalable reporting\n- Provide a clear relational blueprint\n\n ### Final Outcome\n\n Explain the transformation from the original base-table design to the normalized relational model.\n\n---\n\n # 21\\. Data-quality / report consistency panel\n\n Create a small warning panel:\n\n ## Report Consistency Notes\n\n The submitted report contains some numerical/entity-count inconsistencies:\n\n 1. Some sections say 17 or 18 entities.\n2. The final list explicitly names 19 entities.\n3. Inventory and Suppliers are described as entities but are omitted from the final 19-item list.\n4. The conclusion references \342\200\23417\342\200\22318 entities.\342\200\235\n\n The application should distinguish:\n\n **Final Explicit List: 19**\n\n and\n\n **Complete Documented Schema: 21 including Inventory and Suppliers**\n\n Do not hide this discrepancy.\n\n---\n\n # 22\\. Interactive relationship highlighting\n\n When the user clicks an entity such as:\n\n **Orders**\n\n highlight:\n\n - Customers\n- Order Items\n- Order Financials\n- Payments\n- Shipments\n\n Dim all unrelated entities.\n\n Display:\n\n```\nCustomers\n   \342\224\202\n   \342\224\202 1:M\n   \342\226\274\nOrders\n \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:M \342\206\222 Order Items\n \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:1 \342\206\222 Order Financials\n \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:M \342\206\222 Payments\n \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 1:M \342\206\222 Shipments\n```\n\n This should be one of the strongest visual features of the application.\n\n---\n\n # 23\\. Search\n\n Add global search.\n\n Placeholder:\n\n **Search entities, attributes, relationships...**\n\n Searching:\n\n `Payment`\n\n should return:\n\n - Payments\n- Payment Method\n- Payment Status\n- Orders \342\206\222 Payments relationship\n\n Searching:\n\n `CustomerID`\n\n should return every table containing CustomerID.\n\n---\n\n # 24\\. Presentation mode\n\n Add a button:\n\n **Presentation Mode**\n\n When activated:\n\n - Hide sidebar\n- Expand ERD/chart\n- Increase font sizes\n- Use full screen\n- Provide Previous / Next controls\n\n Presentation sections:\n\n 1. Project Overview\n2. Original 9 Tables\n3. 1NF\n4. 2NF\n5. 3NF\n6. Final Schema\n7. ERD\n8. Sample Data\n9. Conclusion\n\n This allows the website to be used during a university viva/presentation.\n\n---\n\n # 25\\. Conclusion section\n\n Create:\n\n ## Conclusion\n\n Explain that the normalized design separates customer management, address management, product cataloging, inventory, orders, payments and logistics into logical entities.\n\n Explain that normalization:\n\n - removes repeating groups\n- reduces redundancy\n- removes partial dependencies\n- removes transitive dependencies\n- improves consistency\n- strengthens referential integrity\n- makes the database easier to maintain and extend\n\n End with:\n\n **9 Base Tables \342\206\222 Normalized Relational Design**\n\n---\n\n # 26\\. Footer\n\n Display:\n\n **Database Systems \302\267 Semester 3 \302\267 Individual Project**\n\n **Muhammad Abu Bakar (FA24-BBD-109)**\n\n **Prepared for Sir Ayyaz Mahmood**\n\n **Educational Database Design Demonstration**\n\n---\n\n # 27\\. Technical requirements\n\n Build the complete working frontend.\n\n Prefer:\n\n - React\n- TypeScript\n- modern CSS\n- SVG for ERD\n- client-side state management\n\n Do not require a backend.\n\n All interactive functionality should work immediately after the app loads.\n\n Do not use fake buttons.\n\n Every button must perform an action.\n\n Implement:\n\n - Entity search\n- Entity selection\n- ERD highlighting\n- Zoom\n- Pan\n- Relationship filtering\n- Normalization stage switching\n- Data table filtering\n- SQL demo execution\n- Dark mode\n- Presentation mode\n- Reset layout\n- Responsive navigation\n\n Use clean component architecture.\n\n Create reusable components such as:\n\n - `Sidebar`\n- `Header`\n- `StatCard`\n- `EntityCard`\n- `ERDCanvas`\n- `RelationshipLine`\n- `EntityDrawer`\n- `NormalizationStep`\n- `SchemaTable`\n- `DataExplorer`\n- `SQLPlayground`\n- `DependencyGraph`\n- `SearchBar`\n\n---\n\n # 28\\. Final quality requirements\n\n Before completing the application, test:\n\n 1. The application loads without errors.\n2. The ERD renders correctly.\n3. Entities can be selected.\n4. Relationships highlight correctly.\n5. Search works.\n6. Entity filtering works.\n7. Normalization steps switch correctly.\n8. Sample data tables work.\n9. SQL examples execute.\n10. Dark mode works.\n11. Presentation mode works.\n12. Mobile layout works.\n13. No dead buttons exist.\n14. No console errors exist.\n15. The 19-vs-21 entity discrepancy is clearly documented.\n16. All relationships from the supplied report are represented.\n17. Primary keys and foreign keys are visually distinguishable.\n18. The website looks like a polished database product rather than a plain academic HTML page.\n\n Most importantly, make the **ERD Explorer + Normalization Lab + SQL Playground** the centerpiece of the experience.\n\n The finished result should feel like an interactive database system that demonstrates the student\'s understanding of relational database design, rather than merely displaying the contents of the report."
    }
  }
}
